{
  "image_name": "1b33946d0de78a234e7e60fe802a9ecd.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares astrocyte involvement at synapses in the immature versus mature nervous system, highlighting a shift from synapse-building support to homeostatic and network-regulating functions. In both panels, green astrocytic processes surround gray synapses, but the molecules and activities associated with them differ markedly with age.\n\nIn the immature system, astrocytes appear to promote synapse formation and remodeling. The extracellular matrix includes factors such as versicans, neurocans, TNC, and synaptogenic proteins like TSP1/2, SPARC, and Hevin, while metalloproteinases are increased, suggesting active matrix turnover. Functionally, astrocytes show signaling through mGluR3 and mGluR5 with calcium changes, increased glutamate/aspartate transport-related components, and metabolic support characterized by glycolysis, pyruvate carboxylation, ketosis, and ketogenesis leading to ketone bodies.\n\nIn the mature system, the matrix composition changes to brevicans, aggrecans, TNR, and lower levels of several immature synaptogenic factors, while metalloproteinases decrease, implying a more stable extracellular environment. Astrocytic signaling also shifts: mGluR5 is reduced, glutamate uptake dynamics are emphasized, and connexin-mediated coupling becomes more prominent, including Cx30 and Cx43, indicating stronger inter-astrocyte communication. Overall, the figure depicts developmental maturation from a plastic, growth-promoting astrocyte state to a stabilized, functionally integrated one.",
